WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.199
Context quality is what determines whether Cloud Code's output is useful or generic.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.439
In this video, we'll look at the ways context gets into a session, what to include and what

3
00:00:11.439 --> 00:00:18.639
to exclude, how deny rules in your settings file exclude files, why token efficiency matters,

4
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:25.200
and how cloud.md functions as standing context rather than a one-time briefing. Let's get into

5
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.920
it. Context is a budget, not a container. Permissions deny rules are entries in your

6
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:39.040
settings.json file that block Cloud Code from reading specified files and directories. You

7
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:45.119
write them as read of a path, like read of your .env file, and they serve two distinct purposes.

8
00:00:45.119 --> 00:00:51.360
They keep credentials out of context as a security control, and they keep large or irrelevant files

9
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.959
from consuming context window space as a performance control. The context window is

10
00:00:56.959 --> 00:01:04.000
the finite text space available in a Cloud Code session. Cloud.md content, tool call results,

11
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
and code all count against it. When it fills, earlier content is compacted or dropped,

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.680
and session continuity means the context window starts fresh each session, but knowledge does

13
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.440
persist across them through Cloud.md, which loads automatically and through auto memory.

14
00:01:22.639 --> 00:01:28.080
Context can enter a Cloud Code session through four main mechanisms. File mentions by path are

15
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.760
direct. You reference a file, Cloud Code reads it, and it becomes part of the active context.

16
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:40.879
At sign references let you point to specific files, directories, or MCP resources inline

17
00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:47.680
in a prompt. Pasting a URL causes Cloud Code to fetch and read that content as part of the session.

18
00:01:48.239 --> 00:01:53.120
And inline instructions are plain text guidance you type directly in the prompt,

19
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.120
which is the lightest weight method. Each approach serves a different use case,

20
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:03.199
but they all share one property. They add to the session's token budget. The more context you load,

21
00:02:03.199 --> 00:02:09.759
the less room remains for the actual work, code generation, tool calls, and response output.

22
00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:16.559
Useful context changes what Cloud Code would do if it didn't have it. Architecture decisions

23
00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:21.679
are a clear example. If the code base uses a specific pattern for dependency injection or

24
00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:28.080
async handling, Cloud Code needs to know that to produce consistent code rather than guessing from

25
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:35.279
file structure alone. Coding conventions shape output quality directly. Naming rules, formatting

26
00:02:35.279 --> 00:02:41.360
requirements, and patterns. The team enforces prevent Cloud Code from generating technically

27
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:48.240
correct code that fails review. Test requirements set expectations for coverage and framework usage.

28
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:55.679
Known constraints belong in cloud.md so the model never has to rediscover them. Rate limits,

29
00:02:55.679 --> 00:03:01.839
compatibility floors, and security sensitive paths include these because their absence

30
00:03:01.839 --> 00:03:08.720
leads to work that has to be redone. Exclusion happens through deny rules in your settings.json

31
00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.479
file. You write each one as read of a path and glob patterns work the way you'd expect.

32
00:03:14.479 --> 00:03:20.320
A few categories belong on that list. Secrets and credentials, meaning your dot nv files,

33
00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.600
private key directories, and any auth config that should never appear in session history.

34
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:33.119
Large binaries, so image folders, compiled output, and serialized data that cost far more window

35
00:03:33.119 --> 00:03:39.919
space than they contribute. And auto-generated or irrelevant paths, which covers build artifacts,

36
00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:46.880
vendored libraries, CI caches, and documentation builds. The framing to remember is that a deny

37
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:53.600
rule is both a security control and a context efficiency tool. A path left off, it either

38
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:59.919
creates risk or quietly wastes your budget. The comparison between over inclusion and targeted

39
00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:06.399
inclusion matters because more context doesn't automatically mean better results. Over inclusion

40
00:04:06.399 --> 00:04:12.800
means mentioning large numbers of files that are loosely relevant to the task. Every file added

41
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.119
takes tokens. When the context window fills, earlier content gets compacted or dropped,

42
00:04:19.119 --> 00:04:24.079
including things that actually mattered. The result is that you've paid a token cost

43
00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:29.119
for files that didn't improve the output, while pushing out content that would have.

44
00:04:29.119 --> 00:04:35.359
Targeted inclusion means loading only the files and rules directly relevant to the current task.

45
00:04:35.359 --> 00:04:42.320
A smaller accurate context window consistently outperforms a large noisy one because clod code

46
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.480
has more working space for the actual task and less irrelevant material competing for attention

47
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:54.239
in the context. Token budget thinking is a practical discipline for clod code work.

48
00:04:54.239 --> 00:05:02.399
Clod.md content is loaded at session start. Before any task begins, a verbose clod.md file

49
00:05:02.399 --> 00:05:07.679
that tries to document everything about the project exhaustively reduces the working space

50
00:05:07.679 --> 00:05:14.880
available before you've typed a single prompt. Tool call results compound this. When clod code

51
00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.480
reads a large file or runs a command with extensive output, those results fill the

52
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.839
context window further. And the window has a hard limit. When it fills, earlier content is

53
00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:30.640
compacted or dropped by the model. Content that was important earlier in the session

54
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.519
may no longer be available later. Being intentional about what enters context is

55
00:05:35.519 --> 00:05:41.200
a performance decision with direct consequences for the quality and reliability of clod code's

56
00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.880
work. Here's what happens when a session runs long enough to fill the context window.

57
00:05:47.519 --> 00:05:53.359
Rather than failing, clod code compacts. It summarizes the older parts of the conversation

58
00:05:53.359 --> 00:06:00.000
into a condensed form and keeps working, trading verbatim history for a summary that preserves the

59
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.920
gist. There are two ways this gets triggered. Auto-compaction fires on its own as the context

60
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:12.399
window approaches its limit, so a long session doesn't just stop, it condenses and keeps running.

61
00:06:12.399 --> 00:06:19.040
And you can run the slash compact command yourself on demand to summarize before you hit that ceiling.

62
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.239
There's one more lever worth knowing. You can put a compact instruction in your clod.md

63
00:06:24.799 --> 00:06:30.720
that tells the summarizer what to always keep. If a task id or a file path should survive

64
00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.920
compaction, name it there. And it persists into the summary every time the window compacts.

65
00:06:37.119 --> 00:06:43.679
clod.md is the primary solution to the session continuity problem. The context window starts

66
00:06:43.679 --> 00:06:48.799
fresh each session, so the running conversation doesn't carry over on its own. What does carry

67
00:06:48.799 --> 00:06:55.279
over is knowledge you've written down through clod.md and through auto-memory. clod.md handles

68
00:06:55.279 --> 00:07:00.720
the standing instructions by loading automatically at every session start. Rules, conventions,

69
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.799
constraints, and architectural context in clod.md are available from the first message of every

70
00:07:06.799 --> 00:07:12.880
session without any manual setup. The practical distinction is that clod.md should contain

71
00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:19.119
standing rules, things that are always true about the project and always relevant to clod code's

72
00:07:19.119 --> 00:07:24.880
behavior. It's not the right place for one-time instructions or session-specific notes. Those

73
00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:31.279
belong in the prompt. clod.md is the persistent instruction set, not a conversation log.

74
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.679
Think of the context window as a budget you're spending, not a container that you're filling.

75
00:07:37.679 --> 00:07:44.000
Every file you pull in buys you relevance or it buys you noise, and the window doesn't care which.

76
00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.959
That's the whole reason for deny rules and for compaction, and it's why clod.md earns its place.

77
00:07:50.959 --> 00:07:56.720
It's the context you pay for once and keep instead of re-briefing from scratch every

78
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.160
morning. That does it for this one. I'll see you in the next video.

